module

public import Sheaf.AlgebraicGeometry.AlgebraicSpaceBlowupPresentation
public import Sheaf.AlgebraicGeometry.AlgebraicSpaceAbsoluteQuasiSeparated
public import Sheaf.AlgebraicGeometry.AlgebraicSpaceMorphismProperties
public import Mathlib.AlgebraicGeometry.Morphisms.ClosedImmersion

/-! # Composition of admissible blowups of algebraic spaces

Source: Stacks Project, Spaces Divisors, Tags 086B and 086C
(blueprint: lem-sfb-space-compose-admissible-blowups).

Blowup presentations specify the descended ordinary blowup on each affine etale chart.
The two cartesian squares over the open explicitly express the canonical identifications
with that open; the last equation says the isomorphism is the identity in these coordinates.
-/

@[expose] public noncomputable section

set_option autoImplicit false
set_option backward.isDefEq.respectTransparency false

universe u

open CategoryTheory CategoryTheory.Limits

namespace AlgebraicGeometry.AlgebraicSpace

/-- A composite of two finite-type blowups admissible over U is one such blowup, with
an isomorphism over the original space restricting to the identity over U.
Source: Stacks Project, Spaces Divisors, Tags 086B and 086C, using Tag 0860
(blueprint: lem-sfb-space-compose-admissible-blowups).

Proof:
1. Apply lem-sfb-space-blowup-finite-degree-ideal-descent to I and J. Obtain d > 0
   and a finite-type quasi-coherent ideal H contained in I^d whose image on the first
   blowup is E^d J, where E is the image of I. Set L = I H. Products of local finite
   generating sets prove finite type; quasi-coherence is checked on affine etale charts.
2. By lem-sfb-space-blowup-chart-properties, the first projection is an isomorphism
   over U. Both E and J are unit ideals there, hence H and L restrict to the unit ideal.
3. Apply lem-sfb-space-blowup-product-ideal-comparison to I and H. It identifies the
   blowup in L with the blowup of the first space in H O = E^d J, on that first space.
4. By the chart-properties node E is locally generated by a regular element a. The
   Rees algebra of (a^d) is A[t], and its Proj is Spec A with its structure map, so
   blowing up E^d is the identity. The product-ideal comparison for E^d and J cancels
   this Cartier factor. Compose and invert the comparisons to get the required
   isomorphism over X. Both projections over U are isomorphisms by chart-properties
   and step 2. Their inverse maps s and t are uniquely specified by their cartesian
   squares; since the comparison is over X, it carries s to t. -/
theorem exists_blowupPresentation_comp_admissible
    {S : Scheme.{u}} (X : AlgebraicSpace S)
    (hqc : ∃ (V : Over S) (a : yoneda.obj V ⟶ X.obj.obj),
      CompactSpace V.left ∧ IsEtale a ∧ IsSurjective a)
    (hqs : IsAbsolutelyQuasiSeparated X) (U : OpenSubspace X)
    (hUqc : ∃ (V : Over S) (a : yoneda.obj V ⟶ U.val.toFunctor),
      CompactSpace V.left ∧ IsEtale a ∧ IsSurjective a)
    (I : QuasiCoherentIdeal X) (hI : (I.val : X.Modules).IsFiniteType)
    (hIU : IdealIsUnitAbove I (𝟙 X) U) (B : BlowupPresentation I)
    (J : QuasiCoherentIdeal B.space) (hJ : (J.val : B.space.Modules).IsFiniteType)
    (hJU : IdealIsUnitAbove J B.projection U) (C : BlowupPresentation J) :
    ∃ (L : QuasiCoherentIdeal X), (L.val : X.Modules).IsFiniteType ∧
      IdealIsUnitAbove L (𝟙 X) U ∧
      ∃ (D : BlowupPresentation L) (e : C.space ≅ D.space),
        e.hom ≫ D.projection = C.projection ≫ B.projection ∧
        ∃ (s : U.val.toFunctor ⟶ C.space.obj.obj)
            (t : U.val.toFunctor ⟶ D.space.obj.obj),
          IsPullback s (𝟙 U.val.toFunctor)
            (C.projection ≫ B.projection).hom.hom U.val.ι ∧
          IsPullback t (𝟙 U.val.toFunctor) D.projection.hom.hom U.val.ι ∧
          s ≫ e.hom.hom.hom = t := by
  sorry

end AlgebraicGeometry.AlgebraicSpace
